{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>And HaShem said to Abram after Lot separated from him.</b> Rashi explained: as long as the wicked man was with him, the [divine] speech departed from him. And the objection is raised here: but was it not already said, ״וַיֵּרָא ה׳ אֶל אַבְרָם״ (Genesis 12:7), and Lot was with him? And it appears to me that this is not difficult at all, for in the first promise it was said ״לְזַרְעֲךָ אֶתֵּן אֶת הָאָרֶץ״ (Genesis 12:7). And this promise is what stood for the shepherds of Lot to mislead them and to say: the whole land belongs to Abram, and Lot will inherit it, and therefore they were grazing their livestock in another's field. And if one goes by strict justice, there is an answer to their claim, for Abram did not earn it at all, since the land was given only to his seed and not to him, and if so, presumably Lot would not agree with his shepherds, and they were shepherds of cattle and not men of wisdom who would inquire about this. But in this promise the Holy One, blessed be He, said to him ״כִּי לְךָ אֶתְּנֶנָּה״ (Genesis 13:15) — from this it is heard that Abram himself earned the land. Therefore this promise was not said to Abram until after Lot had separated from him, so that he would not hang himself onto this promise more than on the first one, and come to steal in public and not turn back for anything. And what was said, ״כִּי לְךָ אֶתְּנֶנָּה,״ will be explained, with the help of HaShem, in the perasha of Va'era, in what sense Abram earned the land." "Another matter: for the fact that HaShem appeared to him, even though Lot was with him, was because he had not yet begun to wickedly graze in others' fields — for perhaps Lot did not yet have sheep and cattle. But after he went with Abram, gifts were given also to Lot, as it is written ״וְגַם לְלוֹט הַהֹלֵךְ אֶת אַבְרָם הָיָה צֹאן וּבָקָר״ (Genesis 13:5), and our Sages, of blessed memory, said (Bava Kama 93a): ״What brought this about for him? His going with Abram.״ Hear from this that before he went with Abram he did not yet have sheep and cattle, until he came with him to Egypt and they gave gifts to Abraham and also to Lot, so that he would consent to the giving of Sarah to him as a wife. And then he began to graze his livestock in another's field, and then he became wicked, and on his account the [divine] speech departed also from Abram, for robbery causes the departure of the Shekhinah, as it is written (Psalms 12:6): ״מִשֹּׁד עֲנִיִּים מֵאַנְקַת אֶבְיוֹנִים עַתָּה אָקוּם יֹאמַר ה׳.״"], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {0 "The references to רוֹעֵי לוֹט as shepherds of cattle 'not men of wisdom' (וְלֹא מֵחָכְמָה) is rendered literally; the phrase may carry the nuance that they lacked the learning to distinguish between legal categories."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:28:16.613639824Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}